# izh_2004q_depolarizing_afterpotential.odex: converted from izh_2004q_depolarizing_afterpotential.ode by xppautX --convert (docs/odex.md)
version 4

# Izhikevich's simple model of a spiking neuron: depolarizing after-potential.
# Izhikevich E.M. (2004) Which model to use for cortical spiking neurons? IEEE Trans. Neural Networks 15(5):1063-1070, Fig. 1, panel Q.
# This panel is the paper's own discrete update (figure1.m, step tau=0.1), a map, because the model's
# differential equations, integrated accurately, do not show the panel's behaviour: the after-potential of a=1 is a transient of the reset that the ODE turns into a spike train;
#   v1 = v + tau (0.04 v^2 + 5 v + 140 - u + I),  u1 = u + tau a (b v1 - u);
#   if v1 > 30 then v = c, u = u1 + d else v = v1, u = u1 (no event: the reset is in the map).
# Input: a pulse I0=20 from time 9 to 11 (steps K1-W to K1+W of the map).
# n counts the spikes and ts is the time of the last one, both set where the reset is (bounds is raised: ts is a time,
# which the default bound of 100 would stop).
par a = 1, b = 0.2, c = -60, d = -21
par I0 = 20, K1 = 100, W = 10
init v = -70, u = -14, n = 0, ts = 0
I = I0*(abs(t-K1)<W)
tau = 0.1
vn = v+tau*(0.04*v^2+5*v+140-u+I)
un = u+tau*a*(b*vn-u)
v' = if vn>30 then c else vn
u' = if vn>30 then un+d else un
n' = n+(vn>30)
ts' = if vn>30 then (t+1)*tau else ts
aux I = I
# the .ode is a map: x' = f with the discrete method is x(t+1) = f
options {
  integration: { total_time: 500, state_limit: 1000000, method: discrete },
  equilibria: { search_limit: 1000000 },
}
